WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.900
Streaming TV shows together has become an
increasingly common activity for couples,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.060
but there are some shows that can lead to
conversations you probably don't want to have.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.340
In the interest of peace, harmony, and your
overall well-being, you definitely want to

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.700
steer clear of watching these shows with your
partner.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.000
The protagonists of Bravo's Imposters are
two men and a woman who've all been victims

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:24.070
of a female con artist who tricked them into
falling in love with her, and then disappeared

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:24.940
with all their money.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.080
As they pursue the woman who wronged them
all, they find themselves conning others along

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.920
the way, but only those who they believe deserve
it.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.480
Meanwhile, their mutual ex-wife is falling
in love for real, putting her next score at

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:36.980
risk.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:38.560
"Will you marry me?"

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.120
"No."

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.200
This is basically a show about how nobody
can really be trusted, and how just because

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.650
you're truly in love with someone doesn't
mean their feelings are as sincere as yours.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.060
And if you're going to watch this with your
partner, you'd really better know and trust

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:56.560
them.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.860
Otherwise, if you end up jokingly asking,
"You'd never do that to me, right?" and their

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.320
eyes shift just a little, you're never going
to stop thinking about it.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:05.580
Is it really worth it?

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.180
HBO's Divorce is a black comedy in which Sarah
Jessica Parker and Thomas Haden Church play

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.420
a middle-aged couple who split up after he
discovers she's had an affair.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.920
Ultimately, there are many other reasons the
two don't belong with each other, but it can

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:22.370
still be jarring to watch a long, seemingly
steady relationship disintegrate and leave

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.540
both former partners alone in its aftermath.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.140
If you're a young couple who haven't been
together for all that long, watching Divorce

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.500
will have less of an impact.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.640
If you're old enough, on the other hand, and
have been together long enough to have felt

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.970
just a little bored in your relationship,
as all couple sometimes do, the show may lead

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:40.860
to questions like "What would you do if we
split up?" or, even worse, "Do you ever think

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:41.760
about getting a divorce?"

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.340
No matter how stable your relationship really
is, these conversations are never fun to have.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.820
Black Mirror, which began on the BBC and is
now a Netflix original, is a sci-fi anthology

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.560
series which holds an incredibly grim perception
of humanity and the future.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.460
While many episodes don't specifically deal
with relationships, those that do make the

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.980
whole prospect seem nothing less than horrifying.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.060
"The Entire History of You" is about a man
obsessed with the possibility that his wife

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.800
is cheating on him, in a world where people
can replay anything they've seen.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.200
"Hang the DJ" raises questions about how much
freedom we really have to be with who we want.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.519
"Be Right Back" is about how technology can
make it harder to let go of a partner.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.500
Moreover, Black Mirror is a show that basically
exists to raise uncomfortable questions.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:26.440
Whether the episode has anything to do with
romance, sometimes discussing hard-hitting

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.840
social topics with a romantic partner can
lead to uncomfortable places.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.120
Maybe just watch "San Junipero" together,
and forget the rest of the series ever happened.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.680
Walter White of AMC's Breaking Bad might just
be the worst husband in television history.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.880
He doesn't just lie to his wife: he lies to
his wife about not being a crystal meth cook.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.930
By the time Skyler White finds out what Walt
is really up to, he's already so addicted

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.709
to the power of being a criminal mastermind
that he can never really go back to being

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:55.110
a good husband to her.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:03.600
“If I have to hear one more time that you
did this for the family…"

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.260
Not every couple needs to tell each other
everything about their lives, but this show

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.100
takes secret-keeping to new and dangerous
heights.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.560
You'll definitely find yourself wondering
what your partner has going on that you don't

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:16.290
know about, and they'll be wondering the same
thing about you.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:21.140
If you are keeping secrets, that’s not exactly
going to make things comfortable for you.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.180
Netflix's GLOW is mostly a pretty upbeat show
about women's wrestling.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.969
At its center, however, are two best friends
whose relationship has been terribly damaged,

00:03:29.969 --> 00:03:32.059
perhaps even irreparably, by a man.

00:03:32.059 --> 00:03:34.890
Debbie is married to Mark, and they have a
baby together.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.600
However, Mark is having an affair with Debbie's
best friend Ruth.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.820
Debbie blames Ruth, and Ruth blames herself,
but over the course of the first season it

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.460
becomes increasingly clear that Mark is the
real heel.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.919
When Debbie joins Ruth as part of a women's
wrestling TV show, Mark is embarrassed and

00:03:48.919 --> 00:03:50.340
unsupportive throughout.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.150
When Debbie reveals at the first live show
that she hasn't given up on wrestling after

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.980
all, Mark's derision for Debbie's pursuits
is palpable.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.360
"I’m trying to keep an open mind, it’s
just kinda silly.”

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.060
This particular subplot is about how you can
be in a serious relationship without even

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:07.650
realizing that your partner is a terrible
person.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:12.120
However, unless that's a specific problem
in your relationship in particular, GLOW might

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.760
be worth watching together anyway.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.030
No matter how dark its relationship storylines
might be, this is a show that does have a

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.579
lot of other cool stuff going on.

